//? Title: Cycle Sort: Minimum Value-Cell Writes, Quadratic Rank Searches
//? Subtitle: Actual placement traces, duplicate-key identities and the original seed-three experiment
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Cycle sort pays for repeated rank searches to avoid repeatedly overwriting
array cells. The comparison-based variant here writes precisely those cells
whose initial keys differ from the nondecreasing target sequence, once each.
We begin with a three-item cycle, derive that minimum and the quadratic search
cost, expose duplicate-key instability, then retain the original seven-key
example and forty-size seed-three experiment. Datamunge's sorting, work-only
and trace APIs share one executing core: the report no longer measures a
separate reimplementation. Logical writes exclude argument copies, held-item
stores and tracing; they do not predict flash erase cycles or device lifetime.
A final cost model contrasts an efficient sorted-input bubble scan with a
write-heavy reversal, showing why fewer writes alone need not mean less work.
)

\toc

> Three cells and one held item

For A=(3,1,2), the ascending target is S=(1,2,3). Item 3 belongs at index 2,
where it displaces 2; item 2 belongs at index 1, where it displaces 1; item 1
then closes the cycle at index 0. There are three changed value-cells and
three writes. The program draws the actual core placements, retaining each
item's original identity. A write means one assignment to a cell of the working
value vector, not the assignments involved in keeping the displaced item.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cycle_sort_cycle.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::cycle_sort_trace({3,1,2});
  if(t.work.output!=std::vector<int>{1,2,3} || t.work.array_writes!=3 || t.work.rank_comparisons!=7) return 1;
  std::print("| write | cycle start | held value | original identity | destination | displaced value | closes cycle |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(80,1000);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=2.4;box.height=1.6;box.font_size=19;box.fill={219,234,254};
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> positions{{0,2},{3,-2},{6,2}};
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<3;++i) d.node(std::to_string(i),positions[i],std::format("cell {}\ninitial {}\nfinal {}",i,t.input[i],t.work.output[i]),box);
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<t.placements.size();++k) {
    const auto& e=t.placements[k];
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",k+1,e.cycle_start,e.value,e.original_index,e.target,e.displaced_value,e.closes_cycle);
    d.edge(std::to_string(e.original_index),std::to_string(e.target),std::format("write {}: place {}",k+1,e.value));
  }
  d.text({3,4},"One cycle: hold the displaced item, write its final cell",22)
   .text({3,-3.8},std::format("{} value-cell writes; {} rank tests; {} equality tests",t.work.array_writes,t.work.rank_comparisons,t.work.equality_comparisons),20)
   .save_svg("build/cycle_sort_cycle.svg");
  std::print("\nOutput {}; final original identities {}; independent minimum {}.\n",t.work.output,t.original_indices,dm::algorithms::cycle_sort_write_lower_bound(t.input));
}
