\caption(The arrows follow original item identities to their final cells. During a rotation, the array alone is not the complete multiset: include the held item and exclude the still-unwritten start cell until closure.)
\alttext(Three boxes show cells zero, two and one around a triangle. Three directed edges place values three, two and one in that order.)

> Derive the ranks and the minimum number of writes

At an outer start s, all earlier positions are final. Hold item=A[s]. Counting
strictly smaller values in A[s+1..n) gives its first remaining target position

\[
r=s+\sum_{i=s+1}^{n-1}\mathbf{1}\{A[i]<\mathrm{item}\}.
\]
\alttext(The first target position is the current start plus the number of suffix keys strictly smaller than the held item.)

If this initial r equals s, the start already has a minimum remaining key and
needs no write. Otherwise, advance past any equal keys already in the target
block, then place the held item at the first different cell. Keep its displaced
value as the next held item and repeat the rank search, closing the rotation
when the destination is s. The start cell is unchanged during the active
rotation. The conceptual multiset is the held item together with A[s+1..n),
which remains exactly the original remaining multiset after each placement.
This explains why the rank search remains valid even though a physical
intermediate array may temporarily duplicate a value at the start.

Let S be the unique nondecreasing sequence of input key values. The invariant
above makes r the first position of the held key's target block in S[s..n).
Skipping equal candidates leaves already-correct cells alone; the chosen cell
has the wrong key and becomes final after this write. Every open cycle has an
unfilled cell in the held key's block, so the duplicate skip stays in range.
A final cell is never overwritten: another copy of the same key skips it,
and a different key has a disjoint target block. Each write therefore removes
exactly one mismatch. There are finitely many mismatches, so rotations close;
then the outer loop advances a correct prefix. The last cell is forced by the
remaining multiset. Consequently

\[
M(A)=\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\mathbf{1}\{A_i\ne S_i\},\qquad
W_{\rm cycle}(A)=M(A)\le n.
\]
\alttext(The minimum logical value-cell writes equals the number of input positions whose key differs from the sorted sequence, and cycle sort writes exactly that many.)

Every correct key-sort must change all M mismatching cells, so M is a lower
bound independent of the method. The invariant shows that this algorithm
attains it, including repeated keys. For distinct items, the rearrangement is
a permutation decomposed into disjoint cycles; a nontrivial cycle of length k
requires and receives k writes, and a fixed point receives none. With repeated
keys, there are multiple valid identity permutations, even though S as a key
sequence is unique. The write minimum above does not prescribe their order.

The legacy API takes its vector by value. An lvalue argument is copied before
sorting, while an owned temporary can be moved. The kernel then uses constant
auxiliary storage. Work-only calls add checked counters; traced calls own two
value vectors, final identities and at most n compact placement records. None
of these extra copies, identity stores, allocations or held-item assignments
are charged as value-cell writes. Physical persistent-memory instructions,
transaction logging, cache writebacks and flash page/block operations are
separate cost models.

> Duplicate keys: write minimal does not imply stable

Use original identities to distinguish equal values: A=(2a,2b,1c). Its key
sequence target is (1,2,2). The middle value 2 is already correct, so the
minimum is two writes. The rank of held 2a begins at index 1, skips the equal
2b, and places 2a at index 2. Then 1c closes at index 0. The final record order
is (1c,2b,2a): equal keys have reversed their original order. A stable target
would instead be (1c,2a,2b), changing all three record positions. Comparing
value-cell and stable-record lower bounds without specifying the requirement
would mix two different problems.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cycle_sort_duplicates.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::cycle_sort_trace({2,2,1});
  if(t.original_indices!=std::vector<std::size_t>{2,1,0} || t.work.array_writes!=2 || t.work.duplicate_skips!=1) return 1;
  std::print("| write | held key | rank begins | equal cells skipped | destination | original identity | displaced identity |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<t.placements.size();++k) {
    const auto& e=t.placements[k];std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",k+1,e.value,e.rank_begin,e.duplicate_skips,e.target,e.original_index,e.displaced_original_index);
  }
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(75,1050);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=2;box.height=1.1;box.font_size=20;
  const std::vector<std::string> labels{"a","b","c"};
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<3;++i) {
    box.fill={254,243,199};d.node("before"+std::to_string(i),{3.8*i,2},std::format("{}{}",t.input[i],labels[i]),box);
    box.fill={220,252,231};d.node("after"+std::to_string(i),{3.8*i,-1.5},std::format("{}{}",t.work.output[i],labels[t.original_indices[i]]),box);
    const bool written=std::any_of(t.placements.begin(),t.placements.end(),[i](const auto& e){return e.target==i;});
    d.edge("before"+std::to_string(i),"after"+std::to_string(i),written?"write cell":"no value write");
  }
  d.text({3.8,3.8},"Input: distinguish equal keys by original identities",22)
   .text({3.8,-3.2},"Output: 2b stays in its correct cell; 2a moves past it",21)
   .save_svg("build/cycle_sort_duplicates.svg");
  std::print("\nFinal keys {}; identities {}; value-cell minimum {}; actual writes {}.\n",t.work.output,t.original_indices,dm::algorithms::cycle_sort_write_lower_bound(t.input),t.work.array_writes);
  std::print("Rank tests {}; equality tests {}; successful equal-value skips {}.\n",t.work.rank_comparisons,t.work.equality_comparisons,t.work.duplicate_skips);
}
