> Why the comparison count remains quadratic

For n≥2, each outer start s=0,...,n−2 scans exactly n−s−1 keys even when it
opens no cycle. These initial scans alone contribute

\[
B(n)=\sum_{s=0}^{n-2}(n-s-1)=\frac{n(n-1)}2.
\]
\alttext(The mandatory initial suffix scans sum to n times n minus one divided by two.)

If a nontrivial cycle at start s has k_s writes, its initial scan already
accounts for the first placement. Each remaining k_s−1 placement repeats that
same-length scan. Writing C for the number of nontrivial cycles and W for
writes gives the exact rank-test count and the total number of rank scans:

\[
R=B(n)+\sum_{s\,\mathrm{opening\ a\ cycle}}(k_s-1)(n-s-1),\qquad
N_{\rm scans}=n-1+W-C.
\]
\alttext(The rank-test count adds one same-length suffix scan for each extra placement of every cycle. The number of scans is n minus one plus writes minus nontrivial cycles.)

For the original seven keys, B=21 and the extra scans contribute
(2−1)6+(3−1)5+(2−1)2=18, giving R=39. For n≥2, W≤n and a nonzero W implies
C≥1, so

\[
B(n)\le R\le B(n)+(n-1)^2.
\]
\alttext(Rank comparisons lie between the mandatory quadratic base and that base plus n minus one squared.)

Sorted and all-equal inputs attain the lower bound, with zero writes and no
duplicate equality tests. A one-step left rotation of distinct sorted keys
forms one full cycle at start zero and attains the upper bound. Thus this
rank-scan variant has Θ(n²) rank comparisons on every growing input family,
including its best case. Equal-candidate searches add equality tests: precisely
W+D, where D is the number of successful duplicate skips. Each search skips
at most n−1 candidates, so they also fit an O(n²) bound. The measured old
“comparisons” column is R; a total key-test count must include both categories.
Empty and singleton inputs perform no scans, cycles or writes.

> A cost model separates cheap sorted input from expensive writes

For a transparent model, charge one unit per key test and ρ units per logical
value-cell write, ignoring copies and other instructions. Then
T_cycle=R+E+ρW. The existing native bubble_sort_work counts actual strict
comparisons and exchanges; charge two value-cell writes per exchange for
T_bubble=Q+2ρX. This is a chosen model, not a wall-clock or endurance result.
The following comparison fixes n=200 and varies ρ. Sorted input lets bubble
sort terminate after 199 comparisons with no writes; cycle sort still performs
19900 rank comparisons. For a reversal, cycle sort uses 200 writes while
bubble sort performs 19900 exchanges, modelled as 39800 writes. This native
bubble baseline scans all 199 adjacent pairs on every pass, including its
terminating zero-swap pass: the reversal takes 200 passes and 39800 key tests.
Its model cost therefore exceeds cycle sort even at rho zero for this input,
and the difference grows with the assumed write price. Sorted input favours
the bubble early exit regardless of that price because both methods write
zero cells. The input structure and chosen kernel both matter.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cycle_sort_cost.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <numeric>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<int> sorted(200);std::iota(sorted.begin(),sorted.end(),0);auto reversed=sorted;std::reverse(reversed.begin(),reversed.end());
  const auto cs=dm::algorithms::cycle_sort_work(sorted),cr=dm::algorithms::cycle_sort_work(reversed);
  const auto bs=dm::algorithms::bubble_sort_work(sorted),br=dm::algorithms::bubble_sort_work(reversed);
  if(cs.rank_comparisons!=19900 || cs.array_writes!=0 || bs.key_comparisons!=199 || cr.array_writes!=200 || br.swaps!=19900) return 1;
  std::print("| input | cycle rank tests | cycle equality tests | cycle writes | bubble key tests | bubble exchanges | bubble model writes |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| sorted | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",cs.rank_comparisons,cs.equality_comparisons,cs.array_writes,bs.key_comparisons,bs.swaps,2*bs.swaps);
  std::print("| reversed | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",cr.rank_comparisons,cr.equality_comparisons,cr.array_writes,br.key_comparisons,br.swaps,2*br.swaps);
  std::vector<double> prices,cycle_costs,bubble_costs;
  std::print("\n| write price rho | cycle reversal model cost | bubble reversal model cost |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(int rho:{0,1,2,5,10,20,50,100}) {
    const double c=cr.rank_comparisons+cr.equality_comparisons+rho*static_cast<double>(cr.array_writes);
    const double b=br.key_comparisons+2*rho*static_cast<double>(br.swaps);
    prices.push_back(rho);cycle_costs.push_back(c);bubble_costs.push_back(b);std::print("| {} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} |\n",rho,c,b);
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(prices,cycle_costs,"cycle",{37,99,235},2.5).points(prices,cycle_costs,"",{37,99,235},4)
      .line(prices,bubble_costs,"bubble",{220,38,38},2.5).points(prices,bubble_costs,"",{220,38,38},4)
      .legend({"cycle","bubble"},{{37,99,235},{220,38,38}})
      .title("Chosen reversal cost model: key tests + rho times logical writes")
      .x_label("assumed write price rho").y_label("model cost units")
      .x_limits(0,105).y_limits(0,5200000).size(950,570).save_svg("build/cycle_sort_cost.svg");
  int rejected=0;try {dm::algorithms::cycle_sort_trace({3,1,2},2);} catch(const std::length_error&) {++rejected;}
  const auto uncapped=dm::algorithms::cycle_sort_trace({3,1,2},0);
  if(rejected!=1 || uncapped.placements.size()!=3) return 1;
  std::print("\nComplete trace cap: {} expected rejection; zero-cap trace retained all {} placements. Empty/singleton sorting requires zero work.\n",rejected,uncapped.placements.size());
}
