// result_end
\caption(Relative costs follow the explicitly chosen prices and actual kernel counts. It says nothing by itself about a storage controller's write amplification, erase granularity or whole-program copy costs.)
\alttext(The reversal cost plot compares a shallow blue cycle-sort line with a steeper red bubble-sort line as the assumed write price rises from zero to one hundred.)

The trace's default complete input cap is 4096 elements. It rejects an oversized
input before copying, allocating identity/history storage or sorting, even if
the input is already sorted and would emit no placements. Zero disables only
this optional cap. Work-only and legacy calls are uncapped; checked aggregate
work counters throw length_error rather than wrap. Results own their values
and identities; they retain no caller views and no shared mutable state.

Permanent tests replay actual trace records against an independent std::sort
target, check unique destination cells, preserve every initially correct cell,
validate held/displaced identities, and reconstruct exact comparison/scan
counts. All 88,573 ternary sequences through length ten and all 46,234 distinct
permutations through length eight pass, alongside 1000 seeded mixed/full-range
inputs, signed extremes, exact sorted/equal/reverse/full-cycle formulas,
empty/singleton/ownership/cap cases and the original forty-size experiment.
The new cases also run under AddressSanitizer and UBSan. These algorithmic
controls need no unrelated external dataset.

> References and reproducibility

- B. K. Haddon (1990), “Cycle-Sort: A Linear Sorting Method,” The Computer
  Journal 33(4), pp. 365–367: https://doi.org/10.1093/comjnl/33.4.365 . The
  paper describes cycle-based methods with conditional linear-time applicability.
  Datamunge's variant repeatedly counts smaller keys to obtain ranks; the paper's
  title does not turn these repeated scans into a linear-time algorithm.

Every block is a complete editable C++23 program including dm/dm.hpp. All
sorting, counters, trace capture and independent minimum-write references are
canonical native APIs. Images are saved locally under build/ and copied into
figures/; exported results contain tables and image paths, not SVG XML.
